NAME
       fixfiles - put the SELinux labels of files back to the policy's defaults

SYNOPSIS
       fixfiles [-F] onboot
       fixfiles [-F] restore [PATH...]
       fixfiles [-F] relabel

DESCRIPTION
       fixfiles is a wrapper around restorecon for whole file systems. It
       gives files the SELinux contexts that the policy's file-context rules
       (including your semanage fcontext rules) say they should have. Run it
       as root.

       onboot creates /.autorelabel (with -F in it when -F is given) so that
       the whole system is relabelled during the next boot, which is the
       same as touch /.autorelabel. restore and relabel relabel at once.

OPTIONS
       -F     Force: also reset the parts of the context that restorecon
              usually keeps (the SELinux user), for customised files too.

EXAMPLES
       Relabel everything at the next boot, for example after SELinux was
       disabled for a while:

           fixfiles -F onboot
           reboot

       Fix the labels of one directory tree now:

           fixfiles restore /srv/web

SEE ALSO
       restorecon(8), semanage(8), setfiles(8), chcon(1)
